    <section class="slide clear chapter-title" id="rdf-messages-chapter">
        <h2 class="shout">RDF Messages</h2>
        <p class="chapter-thanks">Thanks <a href="https://github.com/pietercolpaert">Pieter Colpaert</a> &amp; <a href="https://ostrzyciel.eu/">Piotr Sowiński</a><br><span>For bringing our attention to these issues</span></p>
    </section>

    <section class="slide" id="rdf-messages">
        <p class="slide-source"><a href="https://w3c-cg.github.io/rsp/spec/messages">RDF Messages</a> · <a href="https://schema.org/Observation">Schema.org vocabulary</a></p>
        <div class="columns two">
            <div>
                <h3>Message 1</h3>
                <pre class="code-example"><code class="hljs" id="message-one-code" data-source="examples/message-one.jsonld"></code></pre>
            </div>
            <div>
                <h3>Message 2</h3>
                <pre class="code-example"><code class="hljs" id="message-two-code" data-source="examples/message-two.jsonld"></code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="note">Each message carries an <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/rdf11-concepts/#section-dataset">RDF dataset</a>.</p>
        <aside>
            <p>The <a href="https://w3c-cg.github.io/rsp/spec/messages#rdf-messages">RDF Messages living document</a>
                defines a message as an RDF dataset interpreted as one communicative act.
                A message stream is an ordered, potentially unbounded sequence.
                These payloads report bedroom temperatures at 10:00 and 10:01 UTC.
                Each payload is a Schema.org Observation with an observationDate.</p>
            <p>This is the RDF Messages proposal, not a W3C Recommendation.</p>
        </aside>
    </section>

    <section class="slide" id="rdf-message-backends">
        <h2>Transports</h2>
        <p class="slide-source"><a href="https://w3c-cg.github.io/rsp/spec/messages#stream-discovery">RDF Messages</a></p>
        <div class="transport-cards">
            <div class="transport-card"><a href="https://w3c-cg.github.io/rsp/spec/messages#sse-live-streams">Server-Sent Events</a><br><a href="https://w3c-cg.github.io/rsp/spec/messages#websocket-live-streams">WebSockets</a></div>
            <div class="transport-card"><a href="https://w3c-cg.github.io/rsp/spec/messages#kafka-live-streams">Kafka</a><br><a href="https://w3c-cg.github.io/rsp/spec/messages#mqtt-live-streams">MQTT 5</a></div>
            <div class="transport-card"><a href="https://w3c-cg.github.io/rsp/spec/messages#message-broker-live-streams">AMQP · NATS · Pulsar</a></div>
            <div class="transport-card"><a href="https://w3c-cg.github.io/rsp/spec/messages#jelly-grpc">Jelly gRPC</a></div>
        </div>
        <aside>The native protocol messages provide the RDF message boundaries.
            These are transport integrations described by the living draft.</aside>
    </section>

    <section class="slide" id="rdf-message-serializations">
        <h2>RDF Message serializations</h2>
        <p class="slide-source"><a href="https://w3c-cg.github.io/rsp/spec/messages#rdf-message-logs-serialization">RDF Messages draft</a></p>
        <table class="serialization-comparison">
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Format</th><th scope="col">Message framing</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><th scope="row"><a href="https://w3c-cg.github.io/rsp/spec/messages#turtle">N-Triples, N-Quads,<br>Turtle, TriG</a></th><td><code>MESSAGE</code> between messages</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row"><a href="https://w3c-cg.github.io/rsp/spec/messages#json-ld">JSON-LD (NDJSON-LD)</a></th><td>One JSON object per line</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row"><a href="https://w3c-cg.github.io/rsp/spec/messages#yaml-ld">YAML-LD</a></th><td><code>---</code> between documents</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row"><a href="https://w3c-cg.github.io/rsp/spec/messages#rdf-xml">RDF/XML</a></th><td>One XML document per line</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row"><a href="https://w3c-cg.github.io/rsp/spec/messages#other-formats">Jelly</a></th><td><code>RdfStreamFrame</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <aside>These are proposals and integrations described by the living draft.
            The proposed MESSAGE syntax requires updated text RDF parsers.
            Turtle and TriG also permit the @message directive.
            JSON-LD and YAML-LD remain under discussion. Jelly already groups data into frames.</aside>
    </section>


    <section class="slide" id="json-stream-formats">
        <h2>JSON stream formats</h2>
        <table class="format-comparison">
            <colgroup><col style="width:20%"><col style="width:12%"><col style="width:17%"><col style="width:12%"><col style="width:39%"></colgroup>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Format</th><th scope="col">Extension</th><th scope="col">Framing</th><th scope="col">Multiline</th><th scope="col">Media type</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row"><a href="https://github.com/ndjson/ndjson-spec#33-mediatype-and-file-extensions">NDJSON</a></th>
                    <td><code>.ndjson</code></td>
                    <td><code>JSON <span class="control-character" role="img" aria-label="Line feed">␊</span></code></td>
                    <td><span class="unsupported" role="img" aria-label="Multiline JSON not supported">✗</span></td>
                    <td><code>application/x-ndjson</code><br><small>Recommended</small></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row"><a href="https://jsonlines.org/">JSON Lines</a></th>
                    <td><code>.jsonl</code></td>
                    <td><code>JSON <span class="control-character" role="img" aria-label="Line feed">␊</span></code></td>
                    <td><span class="unsupported" role="img" aria-label="Multiline JSON not supported">✗</span></td>
                    <td><code>application/jsonl</code><br><small>Suggested; not standardized</small></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row"><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7464.html">JSON Text Sequences</a><br><small>RFC 7464</small></th>
                    <td></td>
                    <td><code><span class="control-character" role="img" aria-label="Record separator">␞</span> JSON <span class="control-character" role="img" aria-label="Line feed">␊</span></code></td>
                    <td><span class="supported" role="img" aria-label="Multiline JSON supported">✓</span></td>
                    <td><a href="https://www.iana.org/assignments/media-types/application/json-seq"><code>application/json-seq</code></a><br><small>Registered with IANA</small></td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="example-caption"><code class="control-character">␞</code> RS <code>0x1E</code> · <code class="control-character">␊</code> LF <code>0x0A</code> · <code class="control-character">␍</code> CR <code>0x0D</code><br>
            NDJSON and JSON Lines also accept <code class="control-character">␍␊</code> (CRLF).</p>
        <aside>
            <p><a href="https://github.com/ndjson/ndjson-spec">NDJSON</a> and
                <a href="https://jsonlines.org/">JSON Lines</a> use UTF-8 and
                place one complete JSON value on each line. They have separate
                descriptions and conventions, but the same basic line framing.
                Neither requires a record to be a JSON object.</p>
            <p><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7464.html">RFC 7464</a>
                specifies JSON Text Sequences with media type application/json-seq.
                Each encoded JSON text has a preceding ASCII Record Separator
                (RS, 0x1E) and a following Line Feed (LF, 0x0A). The JSON is UTF-8
                and can span multiple lines or be a top-level scalar.</p>
            <p>The table shows the usual encoded record shape. JSON Lines permits
                its final value without a trailing LF; NDJSON requires a trailing
                newline. All three support top-level JSON scalars and UTF-8. NDJSON
                recommends application/x-ndjson; JSON Lines has no standardized
                media type; RFC 7464 registers application/json-seq.</p>
            <p>These formats define record framing. JSON-LD context lifetime,
                blank-node scope, and message interpretation require additional
                processing rules.</p>
        </aside>
    </section>

    <section class="slide" id="state-of-the-art">
        <h2>NDJSON-LD</h2>
        <p class="slide-source"><a href="https://github.com/json-ld/ndjson-ld"><img class="github-mark" src="github-mark.svg" alt="GitHub"> json-ld/ndjson-ld</a> · <a href="https://w3c-cg.github.io/rsp/spec/messages#json-ld">RDF Messages draft §2.2</a></p>
        <pre class="code-example"><code class="hljs" id="ndjson-code" data-source="examples/messages-with-null.ndjsonld"></code></pre>
        <p class="example-caption line-feed-legend"><code>␊</code> marks a real LF byte; <code class="json-escape">\n</code> inside a string is an escaped newline.<br><span class="proposal-note">Proposed <code>null</code> record · the current profile requires objects.</span></p>
        <aside>
            <p>The context-only
                record updates context state without yielding a message. The proposed middle null record would represent an empty message between the two canonical bedroom observations. The current RDF Messages draft requires object-valued lines and uses {} for an empty message; accepting null would need a profile change in addition to the JSON-LD grammar proposal. A JSON string
                contains a backslash-n escape, not a physical record boundary. Visible
                LF symbols annotate real line-feed bytes and are not source characters. These are the RDF Messages
                draft profile rules; the name NDJSON-LD alone does not establish them.</p>
            <p>The <a href="https://github.com/json-ld/ndjson-ld">CG specification</a>
                remains unfinished. <a href="https://rdf4j.org/javadoc/latest/org/eclipse/rdf4j/rio/RDFFormat.html#NDJSONLD">RDF4J</a>
                also implements NDJSON-LD, with different processing: records are
                aggregated into one dataset. It does not implement this message profile.</p>
        </aside>
    </section>


    <section class="slide" id="jsonld-null-proposal">
        <h2><code>null</code></h2>
        <div class="null-comparison">
            <div class="null-algorithm">
                <h3>Expansion algorithm</h3>
                <p class="null-mapping"><code>null</code> → <code>[]</code></p>
                <p>The algorithm returns <code>null</code>; <code>expand()</code> normalizes the result to <code>[]</code>.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="null-grammar">
                <h3>Document grammar</h3>
                <blockquote>A JSON-LD document MUST be a single node object, … or an array of zero or more node objects.</blockquote>
                <p>Standalone <code>null</code> is excluded.</p>
                <p>Proposed change:<br><a href="https://github.com/w3c/json-ld-syntax/pull/502"><img class="github-mark" src="github-mark.svg" alt="GitHub"> w3c/json-ld-syntax#502</a></p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="slide-source"><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/json-ld11-api/#expansion-algorithm">Expansion</a> · <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/json-ld11-api/#dom-jsonldprocessor-expand">Result normalization</a> · <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/json-ld11/#json-ld-grammar">Grammar §9</a></p>
    </section>

    <section class="slide clear chapter-title" id="yaml-ld-chapter">
        <h2 class="shout">YAML-LD</h2>
    </section>

    <section class="slide" id="yaml-document-streams">
        <h2>YAML document streams</h2>
        <p class="slide-source"><a href="https://yaml.org/spec/1.2.2/#912-document-markers">YAML 1.2.2 · document markers</a></p>
        <pre class="code-example"><code class="hljs" data-source="examples/empty-document-readings.yamlld"></code></pre>
        </section>

    <section class="slide" id="null-roots">
        <h2>Empty YAML documents</h2>
        <p class="slide-source"><a href="https://yaml.org/spec/1.2.2/#914-explicit-documents">YAML 1.2.2 §9.1.4 · Empty documents</a> · <a href="https://yaml.org/spec/1.2.2/#1032-tag-resolution">§10.3.2 · Core Schema</a></p>
        <p>YAML’s Core Schema resolves an empty document to <code>null</code>.</p>
        <aside>An explicit YAML document may have an empty root node. Under the Core Schema, its empty plain scalar resolves to tag:yaml.org,2002:null.</aside>
    </section>

    <section class="slide" id="extract-all-scripts">
        <h2><code>extractAllScripts</code></h2>
        <p class="slide-source"><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/json-ld11-api/#process-html">JSON-LD 1.1</a> · <a href="https://w3c.github.io/yaml-ld/#stream-processing">YAML-LD draft</a></p>
        <table class="processing-comparison">
            <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">JSON-LD 1.1</th><th scope="col">Current YAML-LD draft</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><th scope="row">Input</th><td>JSON-LD scripts in HTML</td><td>Documents in a YAML stream</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row"><code>false</code> default</th><td>First eligible script</td><td>First YAML document</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row"><code>true</code></th><td>Chain script content into an array</td><td>Chain constructed documents into an array</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Array roots</th><td>Append their items<br><code>[A,B] + C → [A,B,C]</code></td><td class="array-root-issue"><span role="img" aria-label="Issue">⚠</span> Different array-root handling<br><code>[A,B] + C → [[A,B],C]</code></td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Blank-node IDs</th><td>Shared within the resulting document</td><td>Shared within the resulting document</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="example-caption">HTML comparison assumes no fragment or profile selects a specific script.</p>
        <aside>The YAML-LD column describes the live editor's draft, checked
            17 October 2026. Shared blank-node identity follows processing the
            aggregate as one JSON-LD document. Array-root handling differs at
            construction, even where later expansion removes array nesting.
            This table does not incorporate pending local specification edits.</aside>
    </section>

    <section class="slide" id="blank-node-scope">
        <h2>Blank-node scope</h2>
        <p class="slide-source"><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/json-ld11/#identifying-blank-nodes">JSON-LD · blank-node identifiers</a></p>
        <p class="variant-label">Both observations use <code>_:reading</code></p>
        <div class="columns two">
            <pre class="code-example"><code class="hljs" data-source="examples/blank-one.jsonld"></code></pre>
            <pre class="code-example"><code class="hljs" data-source="examples/blank-two.jsonld"></code></pre>
        </div>
        <p class="scope-result">Chained: one observation · processed separately: two observations</p>
        <aside>Only the explicit observation IDs change. The bedroom, timestamps,
            values, units and context match the canonical example. The nested room
            nodes are not observation nodes. This compares RDF identity, not the
            number of expanded JSON objects. Preserve independent blank-node scopes
            if separately processed RDF outputs are later combined.</aside>
    </section>

    <section class="slide" id="yaml-parser-api">
        <h2>YAML library APIs</h2>
        <p class="slide-source"><a href="https://yaml.org/spec/1.2.2/#31-processes">YAML model</a></p>
        <table class="library-comparison">
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Library</th><th scope="col">Function</th><th scope="col">1 doc</th><th scope="col">2+ docs</th><th scope="col">Behavior</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody><tr><td rowspan="2"><a href="https://yaml.dev/doc/ruamel.yaml/api/"><img class="language-icon" src="python.svg" alt="Python"> ruamel.yaml</a></td><th scope="row"><code>YAML().load()</code></th><td class="supported">✓</td><td class="unsupported">✗</td><td>Rejects a second document</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row"><code>YAML().load_all()</code></th><td class="supported">✓</td><td class="supported">✓</td><td>Lazy generator</td></tr>
<tr><td rowspan="2"><a href="https://pyyaml.org/wiki/PyYAMLDocumentation"><img class="language-icon" src="python.svg" alt="Python"> PyYAML</a></td><th scope="row"><code>safe_load()</code></th><td class="supported">✓</td><td class="unsupported">✗</td><td>Rejects a second document</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row"><code>safe_load_all()</code></th><td class="supported">✓</td><td class="supported">✓</td><td>Lazy generator</td></tr>
<tr><td rowspan="2"><a href="https://github.com/nodeca/js-yaml"><img class="language-icon" src="javascript.svg" alt="JavaScript"> js-yaml</a></td><th scope="row"><code>load()</code></th><td class="supported">✓</td><td class="unsupported">✗</td><td>Rejects a second document</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row"><code>loadAll()</code></th><td class="supported">✓</td><td class="supported">✓</td><td>Array / synchronous callback</td></tr></tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="example-caption">YAML defines loading, not programming-language function signatures.</p>
        <aside>ruamel.yaml entries denote methods on a YAML instance. Verified:
            ruamel.yaml 0.19.1, PyYAML 6.0.1, js-yaml 5.5.0. Python generators
            construct documents during iteration. js-yaml accepts multiple documents
            but returns an array or invokes a synchronous callback; neither is an
            asynchronous input-stream API. next(load_all(...)) selects one document.</aside>
    </section>

<section class="slide clear chapter-title" id="streaming-api-chapter">
        <h2 class="shout">Streaming API?</h2>
    </section>

    <section class="slide" id="expansion-api">
        <h2>JSON-LD 1.1 · <code>expand()</code></h2>
        <p class="slide-source"><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/json-ld11-api/#the-jsonldprocessor-interface">JSON-LD 1.1 API</a></p>
        <pre class="code-example"><code class="hljs" data-source="examples/expansion-api.webidl"></code></pre>
        <p class="example-caption"><code>JsonLdRecord</code>: object fields may contain arrays.<br><code>sequence&lt;JsonLdRecord&gt;</code>: the result array.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="slide" id="shared-streaming-api">
        <h2>Proposed · <code>expandStream()</code></h2>
        <p class="slide-source"><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/json-ld11-api/#the-jsonldprocessor-interface">JSON-LD 1.1 API types</a> · <a href="https://webidl.spec.whatwg.org/#idl-async-iterable">Web IDL</a></p>
        <pre class="code-example"><code class="hljs" data-source="examples/streaming-api-proposal.webidl"></code></pre>
        <p class="example-caption">URL or document array · each item expands independently.</p>
        <aside>Proposal, not an implemented API. JsonLdDocument and JsonLdStreamInput are proposed typedefs; JsonLdRecord and JsonLdOptions come from JSON-LD 1.1. Input may be a URL identifying a document stream, or an array whose items are individual documents. Each item is an object, an array of objects, or null. [] supplies zero documents; [null] supplies one document yielding []; [[A, B]] supplies one array-valued document yielding expand([A, B]). Thus [A, B] yields expand(A), then expand(B), with independent context and blank-node scope. A nested document array is not flattened into the outer document stream. Empty results are preserved. Errors reject the pending iteration; cancellation remains open.</aside>
    </section>

    <section class="slide" id="streaming-operations">
        <h2>Possible streaming operations</h2>
        <p class="slide-source"><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/json-ld11-api/#the-jsonldprocessor-interface">JSON-LD 1.1 API</a> · <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/json-ld11-framing/#the-jsonldprocessor-interface">Framing</a></p>
        <div class="operation-cards">
            <div class="operation-card"><h3><code>expandStream()</code></h3><p>Each document → expanded array</p></div>
            <div class="operation-card"><h3><code>compactStream()</code></h3><p>Each document + context → compacted document</p></div>
            <div class="operation-card"><h3><code>flattenStream()</code></h3><p>Each document → flattened graph</p></div>
            <div class="operation-card"><h3><code>frameStream()</code></h3><p>Each document + frame → framed document</p></div>
            <div class="operation-card"><h3><code>toRdfStream()</code></h3><p>Each document → RDF dataset</p></div>
            <div class="operation-card"><h3><code>fromRdfStream()</code></h3><p>Each RDF dataset → JSON-LD</p></div>
        </div>
        <aside>These are candidate operation names, not identical signatures.
            Compact needs a context; frame needs a frame. fromRdf consumes RDF
            dataset messages, while the other methods consume JSON-LD-compatible
            documents. Cross-document framing or flattening would require a
            different contract and buffering. They are not implied here.</aside>
    </section>

    <section class="slide" id="cg-questions">
        <h2>Proposals</h2>
        <p class="slide-source"><a href="https://github.com/w3c/yaml-ld/issues/254"><img class="github-mark" src="github-mark.svg" alt="GitHub"> w3c/yaml-ld#254</a></p>
        <div class="proposal-cards">
            <div class="proposal-card"><h3><span class="proposal-reject" role="img" aria-label="Remove">✗</span> <code>extractAllScripts</code></h3><p>@ YAML-LD</p></div>
            <div class="proposal-card"><h3><code>expand()</code></h3><p>Reads only the first document</p></div>
            <div class="proposal-card"><h3>Streaming API!</h3></div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="slide" id="context-scope">
        <h2>Context scope</h2>
        <p class="slide-source"><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/json-ld11-api/#expansion-algorithm">JSON-LD expansion</a></p>
        <pre class="code-example"><code class="hljs" data-source="examples/context-scope.yamlld"></code></pre>
        <p class="scope-result">Context moved to the first document: names, times, readings, and units lose their mappings.</p>
        <p class="context-profile"><a href="https://w3c-cg.github.io/rsp/spec/messages#json-ld">RDF Messages §2.2</a>: NDJSON-LD carries the active context forward until replaced.</p>
        <aside>This is the canonical pair with the context moved from each
            observation into an initial context-only document. All other fields are
            unchanged. A sibling's context does not apply to later array items.
            With a URL base, the relative @type value resolves against that URL;
            it does not resolve to schema:Observation. Independent expansion has
            the same context problem. The RDF Messages NDJSON-LD
            profile has a separate, explicit rule carrying the context forward.</aside>
    </section>

